GUIDE = "# Rollover Execution Playbook\n**Internal advisor checklist | October 2026 | For licensed, authorized NYL personnel**\n\nThis guide is based on the supplied 38-page training transcript, with additional regulatory and execution safeguards. It is not NYL-approved advertising, legal/tax advice, or a substitute for current NYL/NYLIFE Securities procedures. Never prioritize compensation over the client's best interest.\n\n## 1. Qualify the opportunity\n- [ ] Confirm client identity, state of residence, registration/appointment and your authority to discuss securities or annuities.\n- [ ] Identify triggering event: separation, retirement, plan termination, eligible in-service distribution, or existing IRA transfer.\n- [ ] Obtain current statement and plan distribution summary; identify account type (pre-tax, Roth, after-tax), balance, employer stock, loans, vesting, fees and beneficiaries.\n- [ ] Identify liquidity needs, emergency savings, age, employment, tax bracket, planned retirement, risk capacity and tolerance, investment objectives, time horizon, other assets and outside advice.\n- [ ] Identify potential RMDs, age-55 separation exception, age-59½ access, creditor protection, NUA employer-stock treatment, outstanding plan loans, and special 403(b)/governmental plan rules. Escalate unusual cases.\n\n## 2. Present ALL available alternatives\n- [ ] Leave money in the existing employer plan (when allowed): compare investment menu, fees, services, protections and withdrawal features.\n- [ ] Roll into the new employer's plan (if accepted): compare costs, loans, consolidation, access and plan restrictions.\n- [ ] Direct rollover to an IRA: compare investments, ongoing service, costs, creditor treatment, withdrawal exceptions and suitability.\n- [ ] Take a taxable distribution: explain ordinary income taxes, withholding and potential additional 10% tax before 59½, subject to exceptions.\n- [ ] Consider Roth conversion separately only after tax analysis; a pre-tax-to-Roth conversion generally creates taxable income.\n- [ ] Document why the recommendation is better for THIS client than leaving assets where they are, including any increased fees, lost plan features and conflicts.\n\n## 3. Client discovery and suitability\n- [ ] Send the NYL investor profile / IPT questionnaire for independent client completion. Do not coach responses or suggest a higher risk score.\n- [ ] Obtain recent plan statement and any required plan documentation through approved secure channels.\n- [ ] Review existing holdings and proposed allocation with authorized tools, including Morningstar RBT where applicable (transcript process; confirm current access).\n- [ ] Prepare side-by-side comparison: current plan vs proposed IRA vs proposed annuity. Include all-in costs, fund expenses, advisory fees, sales loads, annuity M&E/rider charges, surrender schedule, liquidity and guarantees.\n- [ ] For variable annuity within IRA, explicitly justify extra insurance features: the IRA already provides tax deferral. Evaluate contract surrender restrictions and investment risk. Never claim all fund exchanges are free of charges.\n- [ ] Discuss compensation/conflicts accurately using current disclosures; never claim a transfer is 'free' without checking all fees.\n\n## 4. Prepare and obtain approval\n- [ ] Confirm state securities registration, product licensing, firm permissions and supervision before soliciting or recommending.\n- [ ] Complete firm-required rollover/best-interest documentation and supervisory review BEFORE processing.\n- [ ] Verify exact product and account availability, share class, breakpoints, expense ratios and applicable prospectus.\n- [ ] Complete electronic disclosures through current NYL portal: privacy notice, Form CRS, applicable relationship guide, prospectus and state/product-specific documents. Transcript references CRG and form 24215; verify current forms, applicability and timing.\n- [ ] For a brokerage IRA, confirm current SalesCentral investments eApp process. For an annuity, confirm current eAnnuity workflow. Do not assume transcript screens remain unchanged.\n- [ ] Confirm account registration and beneficiaries, withholding elections if applicable, and exact payee/receiving account instructions.\n\n## 5. Move funds: prevent delays and tax errors\n- [ ] Call current plan administrator with client authorization to determine whether direct rollover is eligible, forms, signature/medallion requirements, processing windows and fees.\n- [ ] Prefer a properly executed DIRECT rollover from plan to eligible receiving IRA when appropriate; checks may be made payable to the receiving custodian FBO client. Follow official payee instructions.\n- [ ] Distinguish a direct rollover from an indirect rollover. Indirect rollovers generally have a 60-day deadline and may involve mandatory 20% withholding on eligible employer-plan distributions paid to the participant; exceptions and restrictions apply.\n- [ ] Confirm whether the destination can receive in-kind securities; many employer plans liquidate before distribution. Do not promise an in-kind transfer.\n- [ ] Track submission date, expected disbursement, check/wire tracking, receipt, good order and investment allocation; never route client money through your personal account.\n- [ ] Escalate stale requests and missing paperwork promptly, with client permission. Never pressure a client to transfer before comparison and approval.\n\n## 6. Close the loop after funding\n- [ ] Verify cleared assets, correct tax character and investment instructions; document receipt and execution.\n- [ ] Confirm client online access, beneficiaries, initial holdings, fees and service contacts.\n- [ ] Schedule 30-day service review and annual retirement-plan review; coordinate RMDs and tax matters with CPA.\n- [ ] Record consent-based referrals or introductions only under NYL-approved rules.\n- [ ] Check firm compensation records separately. No commission is earned merely because an application is signed; payout depends on eligible product, funding, supervision, chargebacks and firm schedule.\n\n## 7. Deal-progress checklist\n**Discovery:** client consent / eligibility / latest statement / goals / current costs.\n**Recommendation:** alternatives compared / best-interest rationale / client understanding / supervisory approval.\n**Application:** accurate account setup / signed forms / disclosures delivered / receiving instructions confirmed.\n**Transfer:** current custodian contacted / request submitted / funds tracked / exception escalated.\n**Funded:** receipt verified / investments allocated / confirmation delivered / follow-up booked.\n**Compensation:** eligible transaction confirmed in official NYL records / no undisclosed conflicts.\n\n## 8. Suggested language\n**Opening:** “When you leave an employer, there are usually several ways to handle your retirement plan. My job is to compare them with you, including the option of leaving it where it is, so you can make an informed choice.”\n\n**Statement request:** “Could you securely share your latest plan statement and any distribution information? I'll compare your current costs, investments and plan features with the alternatives.”\n\n**Funding follow-up:** “With your authorization, I'll coordinate with the plan administrator and our receiving team, confirm the correct direct-rollover instructions, and keep you informed until the assets are received.”\n\n**Fee objection:** “Let's put the current and proposed all-in costs side by side, including fund expenses, transaction or advisory fees and any annuity charges. We should only move the assets if the overall benefits justify the change.”\n\n## 9. Transcript-to-policy caution\nThe supplied training describes four options, the IPT questionnaire, Morningstar RBT, investments eApp/eAnnuity, electronic disclosures and hands-on custodian follow-up. It also includes broad claims about annuity charges, continued pretax payroll contributions to an IRA, probate, fees and tax treatment that should NOT be repeated as general rules without verification. Direct rollover is distinct from a 60-day indirect rollover. IRA contribution eligibility/limits and employer payroll deferrals are separate issues. Annuity guarantees depend on the issuing insurer and contract; variable investments can lose value.\n\n**Sources:** User-supplied “Options for handling a 401K upon separation” training transcript (Oct. 9, 2026), pp. 1–3 (four options), 20–28 (IPT, statement, Morningstar, eApp and transfer), 29–34 (fees and disclosures). General safeguards: IRS rollover guidance, FINRA Rule 2111/Reg BI where applicable, SEC Regulation Best Interest and Form CRS; verify current firm policies and laws.\n\n## 10. NYL systems - operator sequence (training-based; exact screen labels require current portal verification)\n**Control point:** Do not treat this as permission to submit securities business without appropriate licensing, client authorization and firm approval. The speaker names systems, not every menu, field or click. Never invent a click path.\n\n### A. Start the case / client fact-find\n- [ ] Confirm your active appointment, registration and applicable product authorization. Confirm account owner, participant name, contact details and residency in an approved system.\n- [ ] Obtain plan statement, distribution eligibility, tax classification and current plan fees through approved secure methods. Do not upload Social Security numbers, account numbers or financial statements to this Streamlit tool.\n- [ ] Determine whether the case is a former employer 401(k), current-plan in-service distribution, 403(b), traditional IRA transfer, Roth account or annuity replacement. Routing and tax treatment differ.\n- [ ] Record client goals, liquidity, time horizon, other assets, beneficiary needs, investment experience and risk capacity in the approved client record.\n\n### B. IPT / investor profile\n- [ ] Locate the current IPT or investor profile process in the NYL approved environment. The transcript identifies IPT, but does not establish its current exact menu path.\n- [ ] Send the official client questionnaire. Client completes independently; do not influence answers.\n- [ ] Check for missing fields, inconsistent time horizon or risk capacity and ask the client to clarify. Never change the result simply to qualify a product.\n- [ ] Save the completed profile and its date/identifier in the approved case file.\n**Gate:** No product proposal until profile and relevant client facts are complete.\n\n### C. Morningstar RBT / comparison\n- [ ] Confirm access to Morningstar RBT or the firm's current equivalent. Speaker references entering existing holdings and producing a comparison.\n- [ ] Enter holdings from statement accurately, including investment names/tickers, amounts, expenses and cash position as supported by the tool.\n- [ ] Compare the current employer plan with remaining in plan, new employer plan, proposed IRA and any appropriate annuity option. Include plan fees, share-class expenses, advisory and brokerage costs, annuity riders, M&E, surrender periods and lost plan benefits.\n- [ ] Save report and any reference number the tool produces; the transcript does not establish which screen generates that number. If none appears, ask the NYL help desk rather than fabricating one.\n- [ ] Explain tradeoffs and document a client-specific best-interest basis. Escalate employer stock/NUA, loans, RMDs, age-55 exception, Roth basis and after-tax money before transfer.\n**Gate:** No application or rollover recommendation until analysis, conflicts and required approvals are documented.\n\n### D. Choose the correct NYL application lane\n**Brokerage/mutual-fund IRA:** Training names SalesCentral > Investments eApp. Open the current authorized investments application, select the actual approved account/product type, and complete required client, tax status, beneficiary, funding source and investment fields. Check suitability and best-interest prompts; attach current statement and comparison where required. Exact buttons, field names and product options require the current NYL interface.\n**Annuity:** Training names eAnnuity. Confirm annuity is independently suitable and has benefits that justify costs, especially inside an IRA. Check owner/annuitant, qualified funding source, allocation, riders, surrender charges, liquidity, replacement disclosures and state forms. Do not assume a variable annuity is appropriate solely because it may pay commission.\n**Gate:** Never mix pre-tax, designated Roth and after-tax balances into the wrong receiving registration; confirm receiving firm's instructions.\n\n### E. Disclosure and signature packet\n- [ ] Use the current NYL electronic disclosure portal and application workflow; the speaker names privacy notice, Form CRS, CRG, prospectus and form 24215. Confirm exact current form number, applicability, delivery timing and acknowledgment requirements with NYL supervision.\n- [ ] Confirm current prospectus, fee schedule, relationship and conflict disclosures; any applicable rollover, replacement or annuity forms; client signature and date; and supervisor acceptance.\n- [ ] Review every field before submitting: legal name, tax ID through approved system, address, account type, receiving custodian, beneficiary, funding amount, signature, tax election and allocation.\n- [ ] If returned not-in-good-order, read the actual deficiency reason, correct only with authorization and resubmit through the designated workflow.\n**Gate:** A submitted application is not the same as an opened, approved or funded account.\n\n### F. Contact the releasing plan and execute transfer\n- [ ] Obtain the client's authorization and call the current plan administrator with the client as required. Ask whether a direct rollover is permitted, what documents/signatures are needed, whether they send check or wire, processing time, fees, and whether funds must be liquidated.\n- [ ] Ask receiving custodian for exact payee, FBO name, mailing/wire details and receiving account reference. Never guess or reuse another case's instructions.\n- [ ] If check issued to participant, STOP and confirm indirect-rollover rules, 60-day timing and possible mandatory 20% withholding with qualified staff. Do not present this as a routine direct rollover.\n- [ ] Submit correct distribution and receiving forms, record confirmation/reference numbers in approved CRM, and confirm both institutions have a complete request.\n- [ ] Follow up on documented milestones; escalate delays and rejected transfers promptly. Never handle client checks personally outside approved procedures.\n**Gate:** Do not mark funded until receiving institution confirms cleared funds.\n\n### G. Post-funding, placement and compensation\n- [ ] Verify received amount, source tax character, holding account and trade/annuity allocation. Investigate differences between expected and actual amount.\n- [ ] Confirm trade confirmations/contract issuance, client access, beneficiaries, fees and 30-day review appointment.\n- [ ] For compensation, consult the official current NYL payout schedule and transaction records: eligibility, product type, share class, production credit, payment timing, chargebacks and supervision. Never estimate a commission from gross rollover assets alone.\n- [ ] If recommendation to remain in plan is best, document it even if no compensation results.\n\n## 11. Error-handling and stop-sign reference\n- **Client still employed / in-service withdrawal:** Verify plan permits eligible distribution before discussing a rollover request.\n- **Missing plan statement:** Request it securely; do not guess holdings or fees.\n- **Outstanding plan loan:** Escalate for tax/loan-offset analysis before disbursement.\n- **Employer stock:** Consider NUA implications before selling or rolling shares.\n- **Age 55-59 / early access:** Check plan-specific separation exception that may be lost with IRA rollover.\n- **RMD year:** RMD amounts generally are not rollover eligible; coordinate with plan and tax professional.\n- **Roth / after-tax balances:** Identify basis and destination separately; get tax-specialist guidance.\n- **Variable annuity in IRA:** Require documented rationale for insurance features beyond tax deferral, costs and liquidity.\n- **Rejected application:** Correct actual NYL not-in-good-order notice; do not bypass approval.\n- **Missing transfer:** Confirm releasing and receiving institution status, trace payment through approved channels.\n- **Fee objection:** Provide complete dollar and percentage cost comparison, not just fund performance.\n- **Commission question:** Explain disclosed compensation; verify actual payout only in NYL records.\n\n## 12. Self-completion worksheet - one case at a time\n- [ ] 1. Client authorization and eligible event established.\n- [ ] 2. Current statement and plan distribution rules obtained.\n- [ ] 3. IPT completed by client; relevant profile documented.\n- [ ] 4. Four alternatives and tax exceptions evaluated.\n- [ ] 5. Morningstar RBT/current approved comparison saved.\n- [ ] 6. Product/account recommendation and compensation conflicts explained.\n- [ ] 7. Best-interest/rollover paperwork and supervisor signoff completed.\n- [ ] 8. Correct SalesCentral Investments eApp or eAnnuity workflow submitted.\n- [ ] 9. Current disclosures, prospectus and client signatures accepted.\n- [ ] 10. Receiving custodian account opened and exact funding instructions confirmed.\n- [ ] 11. Releasing administrator direct-rollover request submitted correctly.\n- [ ] 12. Request status tracked; deficiencies corrected.\n- [ ] 13. Funds received, allocated, client notified.\n- [ ] 14. Service review scheduled and official compensation status checked.\n**Unverified NYL details to obtain from internal help desk:** exact SalesCentral and disclosure portal navigation; IPT entry point; Morningstar RBT report/reference workflow; current form 24215 status; eApp/eAnnuity screen sequence; product-specific replacement and supervision routing; good-order and funding status screens. These cannot responsibly be reconstructed from the transcript alone.\n"
